  "Question": "If \\(b\\gt1,\\) \\(\\sin x\\gt0,\\) \\(\\cos x\\gt0\\) and \\(\\log_b\\sin x=a,\\) then \\(\\log_b\\cos x\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\log_b(1-b^{\\frac{a}{2}})\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\sqrt{1-a^2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(b^{a^2}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac12\\log_b(1-b^{2a})\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "First rewrite the given logarithm in exponential form:\n\\[\n\\sin x=b^a.\n\\]\nThe identity \\(\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1\\) then gives\n\\[\n\\cos^2x=1-(b^a)^2=1-b^{2a}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\cos x>0\\), its logarithm is defined, and \\(1-b^{2a}=\\cos^2x>0\\) also ensures that the logarithm below is defined. Using the power rule,\n\\[\n2\\log_b\\cos x\n=\\log_b(\\cos^2x)\n=\\log_b(1-b^{2a}).\n\\]\nDividing by \\(2\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\log_b\\cos x=\\frac12\\log_b(1-b^{2a}),\n\\]\nwhich is choice D.",
